            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="p-3 border-b border-cream-200">Year 3</td><td class="p-3 border-b border-cream-200">₹11.7–12.2 lakh</td><td class="p-3 border-b border-cream-200">₹5.8–6.3 lakh</td><td class="p-3 border-b border-cream-200">₹1.9–2.1 lakh/yr</td><td class="p-3 border-b border-cream-200">Minor services only</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-3 border-b border-cream-200">Year 5</td><td class="p-3 border-b border-cream-200">₹8.5–9.0 lakh</td><td class="p-3 border-b border-cream-200">₹9.0–9.5 lakh</td><td class="p-3 border-b border-cream-200">₹1.8–1.9 lakh/yr</td><td class="p-3 border-b border-cream-200">40K & 50K km services</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-3">Year 7</td><td class="p-3">₹6.5–7.0 lakh</td><td class="p-3">₹11.0–11.5 lakh</td><td class="p-3">₹1.57–1.64 lakh/yr</td><td class="p-3">Major services + potential replacements</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>
          For most car owners in India, selling between year 3 and year 5 offers the best balance between depreciation loss and maintenance costs. Selling at year 3 returns the highest resale value in absolute terms but locks in the steepest per-year depreciation. Holding to year 5 lowers annual depreciation cost but introduces higher maintenance expenses.
        </p>

